  "name": "Question 60",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/assets/26f5b15d20d9f57ee9b1d396683dd4dbad06a656b579ac0fd81241d362a28569.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?",
  "worked_solution": "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/assets/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform the function $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y=u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y=u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by two units to the right, giving $y=u(-(t-2))=u(2-t).$\n\n- Finally, we scale by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=2u(2-t).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=2u(2-t).$",
